thread:
lin: Heads-up — I hit a repro where our Java SSE client occasionally emits partial JSON objects. Happens when a single JSON object is split across two read events (small socket buffers + busy GC).

sam: Oof. Do you have a tiny repro or the read loop?

lin: Minimal readloop below (non-blocking NIO):
```java
ByteBuffer buf = ByteBuffer.allocate(4096);
while (selector.select() > 0) {
  for (SelectionKey key : selector.selectedKeys()) {
    SocketChannel ch = (SocketChannel) key.channel();
    int r = ch.read(buf);
    if (r == -1) { /* eof handling */ }
    buf.flip();
    String s = StandardCharsets.UTF_8.decode(buf).toString();
    // hand s to JSON incremental parser
    buf.compact();
  }
}
```

lin: We use a small linger on the socket (10ms) to reduce latency; increases split-chunk probability. Under high load, parser sometimes throws a JSON parse exception because it sees half an object (we expected incremental parser to handle it).

marina: Two quick things: 1) Charset decoding across ByteBuffer boundaries can split multi-byte UTF-8 sequences — StandardCharsets.UTF_8.decode(buf) is not incremental-safe unless you use a CharsetDecoder and manage underflow/overflow. 2) A proper incremental JSON parser is required (feed bytes/fragments, not whole strings).

oscar: Also watch how you detect EOF vs zero-read in non-blocking mode — read() == 0 is normal, -1 is EOF. If your loop treats 0 as EOF and flips parser state, that can drop a chunk. Could that be it?

lin: Nice catch — in my repro I accidentally treated 0 like EOF in one branch when I refactored. Fixing that reduced failures but didn't eliminate them. Still see split objects in the wild when token refresh and reconnect happen concurrently.

sam: For backpressure: are you parsing on the selector thread or handing off to a parser worker? If the selector thread can block or stall, you need a bounded handoff queue and a way to pause selection interest (key.interestOps(key.interestOps() & ~SelectionKey.OP_READ)) until the queue drains. That applies backpressure to kernel buffers.

lin: Right now we hand off to an unbounded executor — under bursty spike it piles up and we get OOMs or long GC which worsens splits. I'll change to a bounded ArrayBlockingQueue and pause OP_READ when queue is above threshold.

marina: For decode/parsing pattern we usually recommend: keep a shared ByteBuffer ring (or per-connection buffers), use CharsetDecoder.decode with CodingErrorAction.REPORT and handle CoderResult.UNDERFLOW by compacting and waiting for more bytes. Feed the resulting CharBuffer to a streaming JSON tokenizer that supports partial objects.

sam: Example decode loop pattern:
```java
CharsetDecoder dec = StandardCharsets.UTF_8.newDecoder();
CharBuffer cb = CharBuffer.allocate(8192);
buf.flip();
CoderResult res = dec.decode(buf, cb, false);
cb.flip(); // chars to parser
// handle res == UNDERFLOW by compacting buf and returning
buf.compact();
```

oscar: One more auth-race note: our current refresh flow closes the socket and reconnects on token expiry. If the reconnect happens while the parser has a partial object in progress, you must reset parser state only after ensuring the new connection's first bytes are not continuations of the old stream. In practice we reset the parser on connection open, but double-check that your reconnect path doesn't try to resume old parser buffers.

lin: Good point. In my repro the reconnect path sometimes calls parser.reset() from a different thread while the selector thread was still processing the previous buffer — race. I'll serialize reconnect+parser-reset via the same executor thread that handles parser mutations.

marina: Also consider switching to a decoder+parser that exposes a "feed(bytes) -> List<Message|Partial)" API so reconnect logic can peek if last item is partial and stash it, instead of resetting blindly.

lin: Tests I plan: 1) force UTF-8 multi-byte splits, 2) force read() returning 0 then data, 3) concurrent reconnect during parse, 4) bounded handoff with OP_READ pause. Will add unit+integration harness that simulates small TCP MTU.

sam: For backpressure heuristics, we throttle at queue size ~ 32 and unpause at ~ 8. Works well for other SDKs. Also add a metric for "paused-by-backpressure" and "parser-queue-depth" so we can tune.

oscar: If you want a quick mitigation while you fix parser: increase socket linger to 100ms and enlarge buffers — reduces split freq but not ideal.

lin: Tried linger=100 — frequency drops but not acceptable under heavy load. I'll implement bounded queue + pause OP_READ + CharsetDecoder + streaming tokenizer. Opening a draft PR after I add tests.

marina: Ping me on the PR and I'll review parser + thread-safety bits. Especially careful about where you call dec.reset() on reconnect.

devex-bot: Reference: internal doc on streaming patterns: https://redwood.internal/docs/devex/streaming-client-patterns (archived top tips + example decode loop).

lin: Thanks all — I'll post the PR and a short repro binary so platform can stress it. :thumbsup:

